{
 "id": "DW-12",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-12-2d43aa8e3f",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 7,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Sometimes when I open the dishwasher there's grey smelly water in the bottom even though it finished draining fine. Not every day though. Kitchen sink was replaced last month if that matters."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Before you start, switch it off and unplug it. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the sink waste / plumbing connection. Take out the lower rack, then twist and lift out the filter in the bottom of the tub. Rinse it under the tap and clear any food, grease or glass from the sump underneath. Was the filter or sump blocked (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the filter is\" — The filter sits in the base of the dishwasher, usually under the bottom spray arm. Twist it out anticlockwise and rinse it under a tap. Typical layout — your dishwasher may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The filter was already clean when I checked it. The dishwasher drains fully at the end of the cycle, it's just the dirty water that appears later. It seems to happen after I've drained the sink full of washing-up water."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Under the sink, look where the drain hose joins the waste. On a newly fitted sink waste the little blanking plug inside the spigot has to be cut out, and the sink trap mustn't be blocked — run the sink tap and check it drains freely. Was the blanking plug still in or the trap blocked (and is it cleared now), or is the connection fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I had a look under the sink. Since the new sink went in, the drain hose just lies on the cupboard floor and goes straight into the waste spigot, no high loop. The sink drains a little slow, but I'm not sure about the blanking plug."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Next: On a new install check the sink-waste blanking plug has been removed. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I'm not sure how to tell if the blanking plug is still in, to be honest. How would I check that? The hose does go straight into the spigot with no high loop though."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "You can usually see the blanking plug inside the dishwasher drain hose connection point at the sink, often hidden behind a small access panel or under the counter. If you cannot easily reach it, ask your plumber to confirm whether they installed one during the recent sink replacement. Check the grey drain hose from the back of the dishwasher to the sink waste isn't kinked or squashed — especially where it runs behind the plinth or under the sink. Was the hose kinked or blocked (and is it clear now), or is it running freely?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can only see the bit under the sink, and that just lies on the cupboard floor into the spigot. I can't tell if it's kinked further back behind the plinth. Do you think the hose being flat on the floor like that matters?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, it matters because a drain hose lying flat without a high loop allows dirty sink water to siphon back into the dishwasher. This explains why grey smelly water appears after draining the sink. Next: Check the drain hose isn't kinked or blocked. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't see any kinks in the bit I can reach, but I can't check behind the plinth. Should I loop the hose up high under the worktop and clip it, or is there something else I should do first?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should create a high loop with the drain hose to prevent sink water from flowing back into the dishwasher. This is necessary because the current setup lacks a high point and allows dirty water to siphon back in when the sink drains slowly. Check the drain hose along its length and where it joins the sink waste for drips, a loose clip or a split. Was it loose (and is it refitted now), is the hose split, or is it dry?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I've looped the hose up high under the worktop and clipped it, and there's been no more water in the tub since. As for drips, splits or a loose clip, I'm not sure, as I haven't really looked closely along it. Is that something I still need to check?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Good news, that has sorted it. If it happens again, start with the same check. Is there anything else I can help with?"
  }
 ],
